  "question": {
    "text": "The difference in the number of unpaired electrons of a metal ion in its high-spin and low-spin octahedral complexes is two. The metal ion is:"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Fe</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Co</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Ni</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Mn</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The electronic configuration for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Co</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>27</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mn>3</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>7</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi></math><br /><br />When it combines with weak ligands (high spin complex),<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,2</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><msup><mi>eg</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,1</mo></mrow></msup></math>, it has <math><mn>3</mn></math> unpaired electrons, whereas when it bonds with strong ligands (low spin complex),&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,2</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><msup><mi>eg</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,0</mo></mrow></msup></math>, then, it has <math><mn>1</mn></math> unpaired electron.</p><p>The difference in number of unpaired electrons is equal to two.</p>"
  }
